    "case_id": "mxh036",
    "case_summary": "A 4-month-old male infant is evaluated during a routine well-child check. The parents report difficulty weaning him from nighttime feedings, noting that he becomes extremely distressed after 3-4 hours without feeding and is only consoled by being fed.\n\nGrowth assessment reveals a decline from the 25th to the 5th percentile in length, while weight remains at the 25th percentile. Physical examination reveals distinctive facial fullness, significantly distended abdomen, and thin limbs. The liver edge extends to the iliac crest, but the spleen is not enlarged.\n\nA blood sample demonstrates a characteristic milky white layer on its surface.\n\nWhich of the following is the most probable diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Prader-Willi syndrome\nB. Congenital hyperinsulinism\nC. Carnitine palmitoyltransferase II deficiency\nD. Fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase deficiency\nE. Gaucher disease\nF. Lipoprotein lipase deficiency\nG. Glycogen storage disease type 1\nH. Tay-Sachs disease\nI. Familial hypercholesterolemia\nJ. Niemann-Pick disease type A\n",
